IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `voc-common`
IMPORT `voc-core`
IMPORT `ctp-core`
IMPORT `voc-seat`

-- Cases for the injured person's seat (voc-seat.l4) and the two cores (voc-core.l4, ctp-core.l4), with the shared
-- arithmetic (voc-common.l4). Every expected value was worked by hand from the deposited texts (the Victims of Crime Act
-- 2001 as at 2021-01-01 and its 2019 Regulations; the Motor Vehicles Act 1959 as at 2024-11-07 and the Third Party
-- Insurance Regulations 2013; the Civil Liability Act 1936 as at 2023-06-22 and its 2013 Regulations), never copied from
-- the encoding's output; the arithmetic is in the comment above each assertion.
--
-- No scenario life raised these Acts: every person is HYPOTHETICAL. So are the CPI figures (round numbers chosen to test
-- the arithmetic, NOT the ABS's published figures) and the State average weekly earnings.
--
-- A breached or still-pending DEONTIC cannot be asserted on the stable l4, so regulative rules are asserted where the
-- trace ends FULFILLED, and #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line where it does not. Clock: days.

`1 Jul 2015`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 7 2015
`30 Jun 2015` MEANS `the calendar date` 30 6 2015
`10 Nov 2015` MEANS `the calendar date` 10 11 2015
`1 Mar 2014`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 3 2014
`1 Dec 2013`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 12 2013
`30 Jun 2013` MEANS `the calendar date` 30 6 2013
`1 Jan 2026`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 1 2026
`20 Feb 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 20 2 2026
`1 Mar 2026`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 3 2026
`10 Mar 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 10 3 2026
`15 Mar 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 15 3 2026
`31 Aug 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 31 8 2026
`10 Sep 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 10 9 2026
`30 Nov 2026` MEANS `the calendar date` 30 11 2026
`15 Jan 2027` MEANS `the calendar date` 15 1 2027
`1 Feb 2027`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 2 2027
`1 Dec 2012`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 12 2012
`1 Jul 2013`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 7 2013
`1 Mar 2018`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 3 2018
`24 Jul 2019` MEANS `the calendar date` 24 7 2019
`25 Jul 2019` MEANS `the calendar date` 25 7 2019
`1 Sep 2019`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 9 2019
`2 Sep 2019`  MEANS `the calendar date` 2 9 2019
`31 Oct 2021` MEANS `the calendar date` 31 10 2021
`1 Nov 2021`  MEANS `the calendar date` 1 11 2021
`10 May 2023` MEANS `the calendar date` 10 5 2023
`11 May 2023` MEANS `the calendar date` 11 5 2023

-- The number of items a list answer holds (a helper for the counts below; not a rule).
GIVEN a IS AN `Application contents answer`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of application items in` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `the application must contain or be accompanied by` items THEN count items
    OTHERWISE 0

GIVEN a IS A `Notice contents answer`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of notice items in` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `the notice of claim must include` items THEN count items
    OTHERWISE 0

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- voc-common.l4: dates and rounding
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- 31 August plus 6 months: 31 February does not exist; February 2027 has 28 days.
#ASSERT `31 Aug 2026` `plus months` 6 EQUALS `the calendar date` 28 2 2027
-- 29 February 2024 plus 36 months: 29 February 2027 does not exist.
#ASSERT (`the calendar date` 29 2 2024) `plus months` 36 EQUALS `the calendar date` 28 2 2027
-- 15 March plus 21 days: 15 + 21 = 36; March has 31, so 5 April.
#ASSERT `15 Mar 2026` `plus days` 21 EQUALS `the calendar date` 5 4 2026
-- Half rounds up; below half rounds down.
#ASSERT `rounded to 2 decimal places` 1.044 EQUALS 1.04
#ASSERT `rounded to 2 decimal places` 1.045 EQUALS 1.05
#ASSERT `rounded to the nearest dollar` 2512.5 EQUALS 2513
#ASSERT `rounded to the nearest dollar` 2512.4 EQUALS 2512
#ASSERT `rounded to the nearest multiple of $10` 2765 EQUALS 2770
#ASSERT `rounded to the nearest multiple of $10` 2764.9 EQUALS 2760
-- 2.25 hours: 2 whole hours and a part, so 3.
#ASSERT `whole or part units in` 2.25 EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `whole or part units in` 3 EQUALS 3
-- The last 1 July on or before 15 March 2026 was 1 July 2025; on 1 July 2026, that day.
#ASSERT `the year of the last 1 July on or before` `15 Mar 2026` EQUALS 2025
#ASSERT `the year of the last 1 July on or before` (`the calendar date` 1 7 2026) EQUALS 2026

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Victims of Crime Act: Schedule a1 and indexation (s 4(2), s 34)
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Schedule a1 has 60 rows; their stated amounts total $2 413 667 (counted and summed from the deposit's table).
#ASSERT count `the Schedule a1 amounts, before indexation` EQUALS 60
#ASSERT sum `the Schedule a1 amounts, before indexation` EQUALS 2413667
-- Row 31 is $32 867; row 60 is $100 000.
#ASSERT at `the Schedule a1 amounts, before indexation` 30 EQUALS 32867
#ASSERT at `the Schedule a1 amounts, before indexation` 59 EQUALS 100000

-- An offence in 2015: no 1 January adjustment yet (s 4(2): "on or after 1 January 2016"); the stated amounts apply.
#ASSERT `What are the indexed limits for my offence` `10 Nov 2015` 0 0 EQUALS (`Indexed amounts` WITH `the s 4(2) proportion` IS 1, `the overall limit (s 20(3)(a)(iii), (c))` IS 100000, `the most for grief (s 20(1)(c))` IS 20000, `the most for funeral expenses (s 20(1)(d))` IS 14000)
-- An offence in 2026, HYPOTHETICAL CPI 130.0 (September 2025) over 100.0 (September 2014): 1.30. 100 000 x 1.3 = 130 000;
-- 20 000 x 1.3 = 26 000; 14 000 x 1.3 = 18 200.
#ASSERT `What are the indexed limits for my offence` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 EQUALS (`Indexed amounts` WITH `the s 4(2) proportion` IS 1.3, `the overall limit (s 20(3)(a)(iii), (c))` IS 130000, `the most for grief (s 20(1)(c))` IS 26000, `the most for funeral expenses (s 20(1)(d))` IS 18200)
-- HYPOTHETICAL CPI 117.37 over 105.2 = 1.11568...; to 2 decimal places 1.12. 100 000 x 1.12 = 112 000; 20 000 x 1.12
-- = 22 400; 14 000 x 1.12 = 15 680.
#ASSERT `What are the indexed limits for my offence` `1 Mar 2026` 117.37 105.2 EQUALS (`Indexed amounts` WITH `the s 4(2) proportion` IS 1.12, `the overall limit (s 20(3)(a)(iii), (c))` IS 112000, `the most for grief (s 20(1)(c))` IS 22400, `the most for funeral expenses (s 20(1)(d))` IS 15680)
-- Rounding to the nearest dollar: row 31, $32 867 x 1.13 = $37 139.71, so $37 140.
#ASSERT `the s 20(3)(a)(ii) amount for non-financial loss at scale value` 31 `at` 1.13 EQUALS 37140
-- Scale value 2 or less: nothing, though Schedule a1 lists $1 000 and $2 000 for 1 and 2 (finding 4).
#ASSERT `the s 20(3)(a)(ii) amount for non-financial loss at scale value` 2 `at` 1 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the s 20(3)(a)(ii) amount for non-financial loss at scale value` 1 `at` 1 EQUALS 0
-- The $2 000 financial-loss threshold is not indexed: $10 000 gives 2 000 + 3/4 x 8 000 = $8 000 whatever the year.
#ASSERT `the s 20(3)(a)(i) amount for financial loss of` 10000 EQUALS 8000
#ASSERT `the s 20(3)(a)(i) amount for financial loss of` 2000 EQUALS 2000
#ASSERT `interest may be awarded on statutory compensation (s 20(9))` EQUALS FALSE

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Which scheme covers me (the s 17(5) join)
-- Arguments: day, criminal offence, vehicle involved, how, terrorist, insurance, on a road, Return to Work payment.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- CASE "Dana", HYPOTHETICAL: hit on a crossing by a dangerous driver of an insured car, 1 March 2026. An offence, but the
-- injury is a direct consequence of driving (MV Act s 99(3)(a)) and an insured vehicle: the CTP insurer; VOCA s 17(5)(c)
-- excludes statutory compensation.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the vehicle's CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act s 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- The same, but the driver fled and the car was never identified: the nominal defendant (s 115).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `not identified after due inquiry and search` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the nominal defendant (Motor Vehicles Act ss 115, 116, 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- An uninsured car on a road: the nominal defendant (s 116).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `uninsured` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the nominal defendant (Motor Vehicles Act ss 115, 116, 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- An uninsured car driven at the victim on private land (not a road): no CTP claim (s 116(2)), so statutory compensation.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `uninsured` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `victims of crime statutory compensation, if you are eligible (Victims of Crime Act s 17)`
-- An assault inside a parked car: not "the use of a motor vehicle" (s 99(3)); statutory compensation.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `victims of crime statutory compensation, if you are eligible (Victims of Crime Act s 17)`
-- A car used in a terrorist act: excluded from "use" (s 99(3a)); statutory compensation.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` TRUE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `victims of crime statutory compensation, if you are eligible (Victims of Crime Act s 17)`
-- A car insured in Victoria: another State's scheme covers it; still no statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c)).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy that complies with the law of another State or Territory` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `another State's or Territory's CTP scheme, not covered here; no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- An assault with no vehicle: statutory compensation.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `victims of crime statutory compensation, if you are eligible (Victims of Crime Act s 17)`
-- An assault at work, with a Return to Work payment for non-economic loss for the same harm: s 17(5)(b).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the Return to Work Act 2014; no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(b))`
-- s 17(5)(b) bars only statutory compensation (finding 24). A courier hurt at work by an insured car, with no offence and
-- a Return to Work payment for non-economic loss: the CTP claim still lies against the car's insurer (s 126A(1)).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the vehicle's CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act s 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- The same, the driver driving dangerously (an offence): still the CTP insurer; s 17(5)(c) and (b) both bar statutory
-- compensation. A hit-and-run at work: the nominal defendant.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the vehicle's CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act s 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `not identified after due inquiry and search` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the nominal defendant (Motor Vehicles Act ss 115, 116, 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- A fall at work, no offence, no vehicle, with a Return to Work payment: neither scheme here (s 17(5)(b) speaks only of
-- statutory compensation, which needs an offence); an assault in 2014 with one: not covered (before 1 July 2015).
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `neither scheme in this encoding: no offence, and no CTP claim`
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2014` TRUE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013 or an offence before 1 July 2015`
-- A crash with no offence: the CTP insurer.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the vehicle running out of control` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the CTP scheme: give notice of your claim to the vehicle's CTP insurer (Motor Vehicles Act s 126A); no victims of crime statutory compensation (s 17(5)(c))`
-- A fall with no offence and no vehicle: neither.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `neither scheme in this encoding: no offence, and no CTP claim`
-- An assault in 2014 (before 1 July 2015), and a crash in June 2013 (before 1 July 2013): not covered.
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `1 Mar 2014` TRUE FALSE `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013 or an offence before 1 July 2015`
#ASSERT `Which scheme covers my injury` `30 Jun 2013` FALSE TRUE `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013 or an offence before 1 July 2015`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Victims of crime: eligibility (s 4(1), s 17)
-- `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` arguments: day; violence; apprehension; sexual; death or physical injury;
-- I was physically injured; psychologically injured by direct involvement; other harm; against my child; relationship to
-- someone who died; I took part; employer's breach; Return to Work; CTP; prisoner; assaulted in prison.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- CASE "Sam", HYPOTHETICAL: punched in the street, 1 March 2026, broken jaw. Immediate victim class (a); violence (i).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- Sam started the fight with a co-offender and was "a party to the commission of the offence": not a victim (s 4(1)).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible: not an immediate victim of the offence (s 4(1), s 17(1)(a))`
-- A bank teller held up at knifepoint, psychologically injured, unhurt: class (b); apprehension of imminent harm (ii).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- A shopkeeper psychologically injured by a burglary with no violence, threat, apprehension, sexual offence or injury:
-- an immediate victim, but none of s 17(1)(b).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible: the offence meets none of the conditions in s 17(1)(b)`
-- A parent of a child who was assaulted, the parent suffering nervous shock (harm): class (c); violence against a member
-- of the immediate family (i).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- The same parent with no harm of their own: not a "victim" (s 4(1)), so not an immediate victim.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible: not an immediate victim of the offence (s 4(1), s 17(1)(a))`
-- A brother of a man killed in an assault, with mental shock: class (d).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `a brother or sister` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- A cousin is not "immediate family": not an immediate victim.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible: not an immediate victim of the offence (s 4(1), s 17(1)(a))`
-- Dana (above) asks this too: eligible in every other way, but her injury falls within the CTP scheme: s 17(5)(c).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not entitled: the injury falls within a compulsory third-party insurance scheme (s 17(5)(c))`
-- A security guard assaulted at work, paid by Return to Work for non-economic loss: s 17(5)(b).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not entitled: a Return to Work payment or damages covers the same harm (s 17(5)(b))`
-- An injury arising from the employer's breach of statutory duty at work: s 17(5)(a).
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not entitled: the injury arises from the employer's breach of statutory duty (s 17(5)(a))`
-- A prisoner threatened in prison, psychological injury only: s 17(5)(e). If assaulted: eligible.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not entitled: a prisoner's psychological injury from an offence in the prison (s 17(5)(e))`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- An offence on 30 June 2015, the day before the 2015 amendments: not covered; 1 July 2015: covered.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `30 Jun 2015` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an offence before 1 July 2015`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for my injury` `1 Jul 2015` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `none of these` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`

-- Grief (s 17(2)). Arguments: day; homicide; relationship; the deceased was under 18; Return to Work; CTP.
-- CASE "the Nguyens", HYPOTHETICAL: Minh was murdered on 1 January 2026, aged 40; his wife, his son of 16 and his
-- daughter of 20 ask.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `the spouse or domestic partner of the deceased` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `a child of the deceased, under 18 at the time of the offence` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `a child of the deceased, 18 or over at the time of the offence` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for grief: not the spouse or domestic partner, a child under 18, or the parent of a child victim (s 17(2))`
-- Minh's mother: the parent of an adult, not of "a child".
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `a parent of the deceased` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for grief: not the spouse or domestic partner, a child under 18, or the parent of a child victim (s 17(2))`
-- The parent of a murdered 15-year-old: eligible.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `a parent of the deceased` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
-- A death from an offence that is not a homicide (not murder, manslaughter or criminal neglect): no grief claim.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` FALSE `the spouse or domestic partner of the deceased` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for grief: the death was not caused by a homicide (s 17(2))`
-- A widow whose husband was killed by a driver convicted of manslaughter: the death falls within the CTP scheme.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for grief` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE `the spouse or domestic partner of the deceased` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not entitled: the injury falls within a compulsory third-party insurance scheme (s 17(5)(c))`

-- Dependants (s 17(3)). Arguments: day; died of the injury; previous order; court finds suitable; Return to Work; CTP.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for the dependants' loss` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for the dependants' loss` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for the dependants' financial loss: the conditions of s 17(3) are not met`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for the dependants' loss` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for the dependants' financial loss: the conditions of s 17(3) are not met`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim for the dependants' loss` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not entitled: a Return to Work payment or damages covers the same harm (s 17(5)(b))`

-- Funeral expenses (s 17(4)). Arguments: day; died because of the offence; paid or must pay; Return to Work; CTP.
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim funeral expenses` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `eligible to claim`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim funeral expenses` `1 Jan 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not eligible for funeral expenses: the conditions of s 17(4) are not met`
#ASSERT `Am I eligible to claim funeral expenses` `30 Jun 2015` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an offence before 1 July 2015`

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Victims of crime: applying (s 18, s 19)
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Sam, assaulted 10 March 2026, applies as a victim: 3 years after the offence, 10 March 2029 (s 18(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `By what date must I apply to the Crown Solicitor` `an application by a victim for the victim's own injury` `10 Mar 2026` `10 Mar 2026` FALSE `10 Mar 2026` EQUALS `the calendar date` 10 3 2029
-- The court extended Sam's time to 1 June 2029 (s 18(7)).
#ASSERT `By what date must I apply to the Crown Solicitor` `an application by a victim for the victim's own injury` `10 Mar 2026` `10 Mar 2026` TRUE (`the calendar date` 1 6 2029) EQUALS `the calendar date` 1 6 2029
-- Minh's widow (grief): 12 months after the death on 20 February 2026, 20 February 2027 (s 18(2)(b)).
#ASSERT `By what date must I apply to the Crown Solicitor` `an application arising from the death of a victim` `1 Jan 2026` `20 Feb 2026` FALSE `1 Jan 2026` EQUALS `the calendar date` 20 2 2027

-- Sam applies to the Crown Solicitor on 1 February 2027 and nothing is settled. Negotiation ends 3 months later,
-- 1 May 2027; he may apply to the court from 2 May 2027; the last day is the later of 10 March 2029 (initial period) and
-- 1 November 2027 (6 months after negotiation): 10 March 2029.
#ASSERT `When may I apply to the court` `an application by a victim for the victim's own injury` `10 Mar 2026` `10 Mar 2026` `1 Feb 2027` FALSE `1 Feb 2027` FALSE `1 Feb 2027` EQUALS (`Court application window` WITH `the first day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 2 5 2027, `the last day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 10 3 2029)
-- The widow applied on 15 January 2027 and agreed with the Crown Solicitor to negotiate until 30 June 2027. She may
-- apply to the court from 1 July 2027; the last day is the later of 20 February 2027 and 30 December 2027 (30 June plus
-- 6 months): 30 December 2027.
#ASSERT `When may I apply to the court` `an application arising from the death of a victim` `1 Jan 2026` `20 Feb 2026` `15 Jan 2027` TRUE (`the calendar date` 30 6 2027) FALSE `1 Jan 2026` EQUALS (`Court application window` WITH `the first day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 1 7 2027, `the last day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 30 12 2027)
-- An "agreed" period shorter than 3 months is not "a longer period": negotiation still ends 15 April 2027 (15 January
-- plus 3 months); first day 16 April 2027; last day the later of 20 February 2027 and 15 October 2027.
#ASSERT `When may I apply to the court` `an application arising from the death of a victim` `1 Jan 2026` `20 Feb 2026` `15 Jan 2027` TRUE (`the calendar date` 1 3 2027) FALSE `1 Jan 2026` EQUALS (`Court application window` WITH `the first day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 16 4 2027, `the last day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 15 10 2027)
-- Month ends: applied 30 November 2026, so negotiation ends 28 February 2027; first day 1 March 2027; 6 months after,
-- 28 August 2027; the initial period (a death on 20 February 2026) ended 20 February 2027: 28 August 2027.
#ASSERT `When may I apply to the court` `an application arising from the death of a victim` `1 Jan 2026` `20 Feb 2026` `30 Nov 2026` FALSE `30 Nov 2026` FALSE `30 Nov 2026` EQUALS (`Court application window` WITH `the first day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 1 3 2027, `the last day I may apply to the court` IS `the calendar date` 28 8 2027)

-- s 19(2), (3): serve the offender unless unknown or exempted. Arguments: day of the court application; offender known;
-- exempted. Sam applies to the court on 1 February 2027.
#ASSERT `Must I serve the offender` `1 Feb 2027` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `serve a copy of the court application on the offender (s 19(2))`
#ASSERT `Must I serve the offender` `1 Feb 2027` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no need to serve: the identity of the offender is not known (s 19(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I serve the offender` `1 Feb 2027` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no need to serve: the court has exempted the claimant (s 19(3)(b))`
-- The deposited s 19(3) is as amended from 2 September 2019 (15/2019 s 5): a court application on 1 September 2019 is
-- under the earlier text, not deposited (finding 25); one on 2 September 2019 is under the deposited text.
#ASSERT `Must I serve the offender` `1 Sep 2019` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: a court application made before 2 September 2019, under an earlier s 19(3) (not deposited)`
#ASSERT `Must I serve the offender` `2 Sep 2019` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `serve a copy of the court application on the offender (s 19(2))`

-- What the application must contain. Arguments: day of the application; not the victim; relies on relationship; victim
-- dead; limited claim; funeral only; special damages or lost earnings; past loss as employee; as self-employed; lost
-- contract; earning capacity.
-- Minh's widow, applying on 15 January 2027 for funeral expenses only: a limited claim, so no formulated claim (cl 5) and
-- no medical report (cl 8); an itemised funeral list (cl 4(1)); relationship (cl 1(g), cl 7); death and funeral dates
-- (cl 1(h)).
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `15 Jan 2027` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the application must contain or be accompanied by` (LIST `the claimant's name, address and date of birth (Sch 1 cl 1(a))`, `the victim's name, address and date of birth (cl 1(b))`, `the date, time and place of the offence, its nature and the details of what happened (cl 1(c), (d))`, `when and where the offence was reported to police, or why it was not (cl 1(e))`, `the nature and extent of the injury (cl 1(f))`, `the nature of the claimant's relationship to the victim (cl 1(g))`, `the claimant's relationship to the victim, the date of death and the date of the funeral (cl 1(h))`, `the offender's name and address if known, the outcome of any prosecution, and whether the claimant gave evidence for the prosecution and if not why (cl 2)`, `an itemised list of funeral expenses (cl 4(1))`, `copies of 2 identification documents, at least 1 with a photograph, certified by a legal practitioner, justice or police officer (cl 6)`, `copies of documents confirming the relationship to the victim (cl 7)`, `copies of documents about amounts received or likely to be received from the offender, an insurer or any other source (cl 11)`, `any medical reports relevant to the injury that the claimant has or can get (s 18(4)(a)(ii))`, `verification of the information by statutory declaration (s 18(4)(b))`)
-- Sam, applying on 1 February 2027 for his own injury and lost wages as an employee: 13 items.
#ASSERT `the number of application items in` (`What must my application contain` `1 Feb 2027` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS 13
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `1 Feb 2027` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the application must contain or be accompanied by` (LIST `the claimant's name, address and date of birth (Sch 1 cl 1(a))`, `the date, time and place of the offence, its nature and the details of what happened (cl 1(c), (d))`, `when and where the offence was reported to police, or why it was not (cl 1(e))`, `the nature and extent of the injury (cl 1(f))`, `the offender's name and address if known, the outcome of any prosecution, and whether the claimant gave evidence for the prosecution and if not why (cl 2)`, `particulars of the special damages and loss of earnings claimed (cl 4(2))`, `a formulated claim: the points claimed for non-financial loss, the amounts claimed for past loss of earnings, loss of earning capacity and special damages, and all amounts received or likely from other sources (cl 5)`, `copies of 2 identification documents, at least 1 with a photograph, certified by a legal practitioner, justice or police officer (cl 6)`, `a hospital report (up to 20 pages) or a report from a general medical practitioner or dentist (cl 8)`, `a letter from the employer or the employer's insurer, or other written evidence, of the period and amount of earnings lost (cl 10(1)(a))`, `copies of documents about amounts received or likely to be received from the offender, an insurer or any other source (cl 11)`, `any medical reports relevant to the injury that the claimant has or can get (s 18(4)(a)(ii))`, `verification of the information by statutory declaration (s 18(4)(b))`)
-- The deposited Schedule 1 took its present form on 11 May 2023 (31/2023): Sam's application made on 10 May 2023 is
-- under the earlier Schedule 1, not deposited (finding 25); on 11 May 2023, the same 13 items.
#ASSERT `What must my application contain` `10 May 2023` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an application made before 11 May 2023, under an earlier Schedule 1 (not deposited)`
#ASSERT `the number of application items in` (`What must my application contain` `11 May 2023` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS 13

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Victims of crime: how much (s 20)
-- `How much can I be awarded for my injury` arguments: day; CPI Sept (year before); CPI Sept 2014; financial loss;
-- scale value; s 20(5) finding; unjust; s 20(7) finding. HYPOTHETICAL CPI 130 / 100 = 1.30 for a 2026 offence.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- Sam: financial loss $10 000, scale value 10. 2 000 + 3/4 x 8 000 = 8 000; Schedule a1 at 10 is $11 000 x 1.3 =
-- 14 300; total 22 300, under the $130 000 limit.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 10000 10 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 22300
-- A small claim: $1 500 lost (under $2 000, paid in full) and scale value 2 (nothing): $1 500.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 1500 2 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 1500
-- No financial loss, scale value 3: $4 000 x 1.3 = $5 200.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 0 3 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 5200
-- A grave injury: $150 000 lost gives 2 000 + 3/4 x 148 000 = 113 000; scale value 40 gives $49 667 x 1.3 = 64 567.1,
-- $64 567; together 177 567, over the limit: $130 000.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 150000 40 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 130000
-- Scale value 60 in 2015 (no indexation): $100 000, the limit itself.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `10 Nov 2015` 0 0 0 60 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 100000
-- Scale values off the Schedule.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 0 61 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: the scale value must be a whole number from 0 to 60 (s 20(3)(a)(ii), Schedule a1)`
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 0 12.5 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: the scale value must be a whole number from 0 to 60 (s 20(3)(a)(ii), Schedule a1)`
-- Injured while committing an indictable offence that contributed materially: no order (s 20(5)), unless unjust.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 10000 10 TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no order: the claimant's indictable conduct contributed materially (s 20(5) or (5a))`
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 10000 10 TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 22300
-- Refused to give evidence without good reason, and the prosecution failed: no order (s 20(7)).
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 10000 10 FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no order: the claimant failed without good reason to report or co-operate (s 20(7))`
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for my injury` `30 Jun 2015` 0 0 10000 10 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: an offence before 1 July 2015`

-- Grief: the court thinks $30 000 fit; the limit is $20 000 x 1.3 = $26 000. $15 000 is under it.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for grief` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 30000 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 26000
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for grief` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 15000 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 15000
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for grief` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 15000 TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no order: the claimant's indictable conduct contributed materially (s 20(5) or (5a))`
-- Funeral: the lesser of the cost and $14 000 x 1.3 = $18 200.
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for funeral expenses` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 12000 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 12000
#ASSERT `How much can I be awarded for funeral expenses` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 25000 FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 18200
-- Dependants: $50 000 gives 2 000 + 3/4 x 48 000 = $38 000; $200 000 gives 2 000 + 148 500 = 150 500, over $130 000.
#ASSERT `How much can be awarded for the dependants' loss` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 50000 FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 38000
#ASSERT `How much can be awarded for the dependants' loss` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 200000 FALSE EQUALS `the award is` 130000
#ASSERT `How much can be awarded for the dependants' loss` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 50000 TRUE EQUALS `no order: the claimant failed without good reason to report or co-operate (s 20(7))`

-- In all (s 20(3)(c)): Minh's widow, injured herself in the attack ($90 000), with grief ($26 000), as the dependants'
-- representative ($50 000) and for the funeral ($18 200). Her own capacities: 90 000 + 26 000 = 116 000, under 130 000;
-- the dependants' capacity separately, 50 000; funeral expenses not counted: 116 000 + 50 000 + 18 200 = 184 200.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded in all` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 90000 26000 50000 18200 EQUALS 184200
-- 120 000 + 26 000 = 146 000 in her own capacities, limited to 130 000; plus $10 000 funeral: 140 000.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded in all` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 120000 26000 0 10000 EQUALS 140000
-- The reading of qualification 1 (finding 26): $120 000 for injury, $26 000 for grief and $50 000 as the dependants'
-- representative. Her own capacities together, 146 000, limited to 130 000; the dependants' capacity separately, 50 000:
-- 180 000. (Capping injury and grief separately would give 120 000 + 26 000 + 50 000 = 196 000.)
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be awarded in all` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 120000 26000 50000 0 EQUALS 180000

-- Legal costs (s 25(1), Schedule 2), proceedings commenced 1 March 2026 at 1.30. Arguments: day; CPI; CPI 2014; kind;
-- offender known; agreed; court application; hearing hours; disability settlement; appeal.
-- Sam's claim at court, a 7.5-hour hearing: solicitor $1 400 x 1.3 = 1 820; counsel $1 000 x 1.3 = 1 300 for the
-- preparation and first 5 hours, and 2.5 more hours (3 hours or parts) x $200 x 1.3 = 3 x 260 = 780: 3 900.
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 `any other claim` TRUE FALSE TRUE 7.5 FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the prescribed amount, before GST, is` 3900
-- A limited claim with the offender known and compensation agreed: $700 x 1.3 = 910.
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 `a limited claim (grief, funeral expenses or both)` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the prescribed amount, before GST, is` 910
-- A limited claim where the offender is unknown: Schedule 2 clause 2(1) prescribes nothing (finding 9).
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 `a limited claim (grief, funeral expenses or both)` FALSE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: Schedule 2 clause 2(1) prescribes no amount for a limited claim unless the offender is known and compensation is agreed`
-- The first of related claims, for a child whose settlement needs approval: 1 820 + $350 x 1.3 = 455 + counsel's
-- opinion on a related claim $500 x 1.3 = 650: 2 925.
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `1 Mar 2026` 130 100 `the first of related claims` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the prescribed amount, before GST, is` 2925
-- Another of related claims, with an appeal, proceedings commenced on 10 November 2015 (finding 20, a correction): s 25(1)
-- takes the amounts prescribed "as at the time of commencement of the proceedings", and then the Victims of Crime
-- (Statutory Compensation) Regulations 2004 applied; the deposited 2019 Regulations commenced on 25.7.2019 and revoked
-- them. The 2004 amounts are not deposited: not covered. Likewise proceedings on 1 March 2018 and on 24 July 2019.
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `10 Nov 2015` 0 0 `one of the other related claims` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: proceedings commenced before 25 July 2019, under the 2004 Regulations (not deposited)`
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `1 Mar 2018` 130 100 `one of the other related claims` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: proceedings commenced before 25 July 2019, under the 2004 Regulations (not deposited)`
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `24 Jul 2019` 110 100 `one of the other related claims` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: proceedings commenced before 25 July 2019, under the 2004 Regulations (not deposited)`
-- The same commenced on 25 July 2019, the first day of the 2019 Regulations: the 1 January 2019 adjustment, at a
-- HYPOTHETICAL CPI of 110.0 (September 2018) over 100.0 (September 2014) = 1.10. Solicitor $1 100 x 1.1 = 1 210; appeal
-- $700 x 1.1 = 770: 1 980.
#ASSERT `What is the most my lawyer may charge` `25 Jul 2019` 110 100 `one of the other related claims` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the prescribed amount, before GST, is` 1980

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- Victims of crime: regulative rules (voc-core.l4)
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- s 18(1)-(4): 1 096 days to the last day (10 March 2026 to 10 March 2029, with 29 February 2028). A complete, verified
-- application on day 100 is in time.
#ASSERT `days from` `10 Mar 2026` `to` (`the calendar date` 10 3 2029) EQUALS 1096
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 18(1)-(4) -- the claimant must apply to the Crown Solicitor, with the prescribed contents verified by statutory declaration, within the initial application period` 1096) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `apply to the Crown Solicitor, with the information and documents required, verified by statutory declaration` 100)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the claimant, "s 18(1)-(4): ..." (an application not verified by statutory declaration does not count)
#TRACE `s 18(1)-(4) -- the claimant must apply to the Crown Solicitor, with the prescribed contents verified by statutory declaration, within the initial application period` 1096 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the claimant` DOES `apply to the Crown Solicitor without the required information, documents or statutory declaration` AT 100
    (WAITUNTIL 1100)
-- expect: BREACH BY the claimant, "s 18(1)-(4): ..." (the application on day 1 097 is out of time)
#TRACE `s 18(1)-(4) -- the claimant must apply to the Crown Solicitor, with the prescribed contents verified by statutory declaration, within the initial application period` 1096 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the claimant` DOES `apply to the Crown Solicitor, with the information and documents required, verified by statutory declaration` AT 1097

-- s 18(5), (6), s 19(2). Clock from the end of the period for negotiation; 679 days to 10 March 2029 from 1 May 2027.
#ASSERT `days from` (`the calendar date` 1 5 2027) `to` (`the calendar date` 10 3 2029) EQUALS 679
-- Settled: nothing more is owed.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 18(5), (6), s 19(2) -- if the claim is not settled, the claimant must apply to the court by the s 18(6) day, and then serve the offender` TRUE 679 TRUE FALSE) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL 700)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- Not settled: Sam applies on day 30 and serves the known offender on day 40.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 18(5), (6), s 19(2) -- if the claim is not settled, the claimant must apply to the court by the s 18(6) day, and then serve the offender` FALSE 679 TRUE FALSE) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `apply to the court for an order for statutory compensation` 30), (EVENT `the claimant` `serve a copy of the court application on the offender` 40)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- The offender is unknown: applying is enough.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 18(5), (6), s 19(2) -- if the claim is not settled, the claimant must apply to the court by the s 18(6) day, and then serve the offender` FALSE 679 FALSE FALSE) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `apply to the court for an order for statutory compensation` 30)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: still owed: the claimant MUST serve a copy of the court application on the offender (no time is stated)
#TRACE `s 18(5), (6), s 19(2) -- if the claim is not settled, the claimant must apply to the court by the s 18(6) day, and then serve the offender` FALSE 679 TRUE FALSE AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the claimant` DOES `apply to the court for an order for statutory compensation` AT 30
-- expect: BREACH BY the claimant, "s 18(6): ..." (the court application on day 680 is out of time)
#TRACE `s 18(5), (6), s 19(2) -- if the claim is not settled, the claimant must apply to the court by the s 18(6) day, and then serve the offender` FALSE 679 TRUE FALSE AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the claimant` DOES `apply to the court for an order for statutory compensation` AT 680

-- s 21(1): a required examination must be attended; none required, nothing owed.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 21(1) -- a claimant must submit to a medical examination if the Crown requires it, at the Crown's cost` TRUE) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `submit to a medical examination by a medical practitioner nominated by the Crown` 14)) EQUALS FULFILLED
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 21(1) -- a claimant must submit to a medical examination if the Crown requires it, at the Crown's cost` FALSE) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL 30)) EQUALS FULFILLED

-- =====================================================================================================================
-- CTP: whom to notify, by when, with what (MV Act Part 4, the Third Party Insurance Regulations)
-- `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` arguments: day; how; terrorist; insurance; on a road.
-- =====================================================================================================================

-- CASE "Dana" again: an insured car, so its CTP insurer.
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE EQUALS `the vehicle's CTP insurer (s 126A(1))`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `not identified after due inquiry and search` TRUE EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `uninsured` TRUE EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `uninsured` FALSE EQUALS `no CTP claim: an uninsured vehicle that was not on a road (s 116(2))`
-- A cyclist who rode into a parked car on a road: s 99(3)(c).
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of a person travelling on a road colliding with the vehicle while it was stationary, or avoiding such a collision` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE EQUALS `the vehicle's CTP insurer (s 126A(1))`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE EQUALS `no CTP claim: the injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99(3), (3a))`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `1 Mar 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `covered by other insurance that s 116(1)(a)-(d) accepts` TRUE EQUALS `not covered: the other insurance s 116(1)(a)-(d) accepts`
#ASSERT `Whom must I give my notice of claim to` `30 Jun 2013` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE EQUALS `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

-- By when: Dana's accident on 31 August 2026, an insured car: within 6 months, by 28 February 2027 (reg 4(1)(b)).
#ASSERT `By when must I give notice of my claim` `31 Aug 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE `31 Aug 2026` EQUALS `within 6 months after the accident, that is on or before` (`the calendar date` 28 2 2027)
-- A hit-and-run on 31 August 2026; on 10 September police said the car could not be traced (reg 4(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `By when must I give notice of my claim` `31 Aug 2026` `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle` FALSE `not identified after due inquiry and search` TRUE `10 Sep 2026` EQUALS `as soon as reasonably practicable after it became apparent that the vehicle could not be identified or was uninsured, which was on` `10 Sep 2026`
#ASSERT `By when must I give notice of my claim` `31 Aug 2026` `in some other way` FALSE `insured under a policy under Part 4` TRUE `31 Aug 2026` EQUALS `no notice under s 126A: there is no CTP claim against an insurer or the nominal defendant under this Part`

-- What to send. Arguments: reported to police; lost income; medical expenses; death; driving.
-- A driver, the crash reported, claiming lost income and medical expenses: 8 items.
#ASSERT `What must my notice of claim include` `10 Sep 2026` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the notice of claim must include` (LIST `a notice of claim in the manner and form the Minister approves (s 126A(2)(b))`, `a statement setting out the details of the claim (s 126A(2)(c)(i))`, `a medical practitioner's certificate or opinion of the nature and probable cause of the death or injury, in the CTP Regulator's form (s 126A(2)(c)(ii), reg 5(1))`, `the police report number of the report made under the Road Traffic Act 1961 (s 126A(2)(c)(iii))`, `evidence of income or earning capacity, such as payslips, PAYG payment summaries or income tax returns (reg 5(2)(a))`, `evidence of the medical expenses incurred up to the date of the claim, such as accounts or receipts (reg 5(2)(b))`, `the claimant's driver's licence number (reg 5(2)(d))`, `a statement in the CTP Regulator's form authorising access to records and other information relevant to the claim (s 126A(2)(d), reg 6)`)
-- Dana, a pedestrian, not reported, claiming nothing economic: 5 items, with proof of identity.
#ASSERT `What must my notice of claim include` `10 Sep 2026` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the notice of claim must include` (LIST `a notice of claim in the manner and form the Minister approves (s 126A(2)(b))`, `a statement setting out the details of the claim (s 126A(2)(c)(i))`, `a medical practitioner's certificate or opinion of the nature and probable cause of the death or injury, in the CTP Regulator's form (s 126A(2)(c)(ii), reg 5(1))`, `proof of the claimant's identity (reg 5(2)(e))`, `a statement in the CTP Regulator's form authorising access to records and other information relevant to the claim (s 126A(2)(d), reg 6)`)
-- A claim for a death by a claimant who was not driving, the crash reported: the form, the statement, the medical
-- certificate, the police report number, the certificate of death, proof of identity and the access authority: 7.
#ASSERT `the number of notice items in` (`What must my notice of claim include` `10 Sep 2026` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS 7

-- s 126A(6), reg 4(2), (3). Arguments: approved form; everything required; access authority; why late; prejudiced.
#ASSERT (NOT (`May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE `it was not late` FALSE))
#ASSERT `Can I start or continue proceedings` TRUE TRUE TRUE `it was not late` FALSE
-- No access authority: the insurer may decline, and no proceedings until it is given.
#ASSERT `May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` TRUE TRUE FALSE `it was not late` FALSE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can I start or continue proceedings` TRUE TRUE FALSE `it was not late` FALSE))
-- Late because Dana was in hospital, the assessment not prejudiced: excused (reg 4(2)).
#ASSERT (NOT (`May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE `inability of the claimant on account of injury` FALSE))
-- Late through ignorance, but the assessment was substantially prejudiced: not excused; the insurer may decline, yet
-- proceedings may still be brought, everything in (2)(c) and (d) having been given (reg 4(3)).
#ASSERT `May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE `ignorance or mistake of the claimant` TRUE
#ASSERT `Can I start or continue proceedings` TRUE TRUE TRUE `ignorance or mistake of the claimant` TRUE
-- Late with no reg 4(2) cause: the same.
#ASSERT `May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE `none of the reg 4(2)(a) causes` FALSE
-- Not in the approved form: the insurer may decline; proceedings are not barred (reg 4(3) needs only (2)(c) and (d)).
#ASSERT `May the insurer refuse to deal with my claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE `it was not late` FALSE
#ASSERT `Can I start or continue proceedings` FALSE TRUE TRUE `it was not late` FALSE

-- s 126A(4): the insurer received Dana's hospital records on 15 March 2026: a copy is due by 5 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By when must the insurer send me a copy` `15 Mar 2026` EQUALS `the calendar date` 5 4 2026
-- ss 115(4), 116(5): late, the defence prejudiced and justice requiring it: the court may dismiss. On time: no.
#ASSERT `Can my action be dismissed for late notice` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my action be dismissed for late notice` TRUE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Can my action be dismissed for late notice` FALSE FALSE TRUE))

-- CTP regulative rules (ctp-core.l4). s 126A(1), reg 4(1)(b): 181 days from 31 August 2026 to 28 February 2027.
#ASSERT `days from` `31 Aug 2026` `to` (`the calendar date` 28 2 2027) EQUALS 181
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 126A(1), (2), reg 4 -- the claimant must give the insurer or the nominal defendant a complying notice of claim within the prescribed time` `the insurer` 181 FALSE) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` (`give a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)` `the insurer`) 30)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- Late but excused (reg 4(2)): once the deadline is seen to pass (day 182), the notice is still owed, with no time.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`s 126A(1), (2), reg 4 -- the claimant must give the insurer or the nominal defendant a complying notice of claim within the prescribed time` `the insurer` 181 TRUE) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL 182), (EVENT `the claimant` (`give a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)` `the insurer`) 200)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the claimant, "s 126A(2)(a), reg 4(1): ..." (notice sent to the nominal defendant, not the insurer)
#TRACE `s 126A(1), (2), reg 4 -- the claimant must give the insurer or the nominal defendant a complying notice of claim within the prescribed time` `the insurer` 181 FALSE AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the claimant` DOES `give a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)` `the nominal defendant` AT 30
    (WAITUNTIL 200)
-- expect: BREACH BY the claimant, "s 126A(2)(a), reg 4(1): ..." (no excuse; late)
#TRACE `s 126A(1), (2), reg 4 -- the claimant must give the insurer or the nominal defendant a complying notice of claim within the prescribed time` `the insurer` 181 FALSE AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 182)

